  "contracts": {
    "lem-qft-finite-instrument-normalization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-qft-finite-bounded-kraus-instrument",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a nonzero complex Hilbert space with inner product linear in its first argument ([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]). Write $\\mathcal B(H)$ for the bounded linear operators on $H$ ([[def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators]]); for $M\\in\\mathcal B(H)$, $M^*$ is its Hilbert adjoint, characterized by $\\langle Mx,y\\rangle=\\langle x,M^*y\\rangle$ ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]).\n\nA **finite bounded Kraus instrument datum** on $H$ consists of an integer $m\\ge1$, integers $q_a\\ge0$ for $a=1,\\ldots,m$, and bounded operators $M_{a,r}\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ for $r=1,\\ldots,q_a$. It is **normalized** when\n\n$$\\sum_{a=1}^{m}\\sum_{r=1}^{q_a}M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}=I_H.$$\n\nEmpty inner sums are zero. For each outcome $a$, define its outcome operator and operation by\n\n$$E_a:=\\sum_{r=1}^{q_a}M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r},\\qquad \\mathcal J_a(X):=\\sum_{r=1}^{q_a}M_{a,r}XM_{a,r}^*\\quad(X\\in\\mathcal B(H)).$$\n\nThese formulas take values in $\\mathcal B(H)$: each composition is bounded since $\\|M_{a,r}XM_{a,r}^*x\\|\\le\\|M_{a,r}\\|\\,\\|X\\|\\,\\|M_{a,r}^*\\|\\,\\|x\\|$, and the sums are finite. This definition specifies only the displayed finite Kraus form; it is not a representation theorem for arbitrary instruments and does not assert that an apparatus realizes these data.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) and let $H$ be a nonzero complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]). Recall that $T_{u,v}\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ denotes the rank-one operator $T_{u,v}x=\\langle x,v\\rangle u$ ([[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]).\n\n(a) A **density operator** on $H$ is a self-adjoint positive trace-class operator $\\rho$ with $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=1$ ([[def-trace-class-operator]], [[def-trace-of-a-trace-class-operator]], [[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]]). If $(u_n)_{n\\in J}$ is a finite or countable orthonormal family, with $J$ a finite initial segment of the positive integers or all positive integers, and $w_n\\ge0$ with $\\sum_nw_n=1$, then the sum $\\rho:=\\sum_nw_nT_{u_n,u_n}$ converges in trace norm to a density operator with $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=\\sum_nw_n=1$. Conversely every density operator $\\rho$ admits such an expansion whose weights are its positive eigenvalues counted with multiplicity and whose vectors are orthonormal eigenvectors; distinct expansions of the same $\\rho$ exist when a positive eigenspace has dimension at least two.\n\n(b) Let $E$ be a projection-valued measure on a measurable space $(X,\\Sigma)$ acting on $H$ ([[def-projection-valued-measure]]). Then $\\mu_\\rho(B):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho E(B))$ is a well-defined probability measure on $(X,\\Sigma)$ ([[def-measure]], [[def-probability-measure]]); it is given by the absolutely convergent series $\\mu_\\rho(B)=\\sum_nw_n\\|E(B)u_n\\|^2$ in every expansion of (a), and reduces to $\\mu_\\rho(B)=\\|E(B)u\\|^2$ for a pure density operator $\\rho=T_{u,u}$ with $\\|u\\|=1$.\n\n(c) Assume in addition the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $A$ be a self-adjoint operator with projection-valued measure $E_A$ ([[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]]) and let $\\psi\\in H$ have $\\|\\psi\\|=1$ and $\\psi\\in D(A)$. Here $|A|^{1/2}$ denotes the unbounded PVM integral $f(E_A)$ for $f(\\lambda)=|\\lambda|^{1/2}$, with domain $D(f(E_A))=\\{\\psi:\\int |\\lambda|\\,dE_{A,\\psi}<\\infty\\}$ as in [[def-unbounded-integral-against-a-pvm]]. Then\n\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda\\,dE_{A,\\psi}(\\lambda)=\\langle A\\psi,\\psi\\rangle,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\lambda^2\\,dE_{A,\\psi}(\\lambda)=\\|A\\psi\\|^2 ,$$\n\nwhere $E_{A,\\psi}(B)=\\langle E_A(B)\\psi,\\psi\\rangle$ ([[lem-scalar-and-complex-measures-from-a-pvm]]); moreover $\\int_{\\mathbb R}|\\lambda|\\,dE_{A,\\psi}(\\lambda)<\\infty$ exactly when $\\psi\\in D(|A|^{1/2})$, and then the mean $\\int\\lambda\\,dE_{A,\\psi}$ is finite. No product $A\\rho$ of an unbounded operator with a density operator is used, and no physical interpretation of $\\mu_\\rho$ or of the moments is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-nuclear-bounded-cyclicity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and for $u,v\\in H$ let $T_{u,v}x=\\langle x,v\\rangle u$ ([[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]).\n\n(a) For every bounded $B\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]),\n\n$$BT_{u,v}=T_{Bu,v},\\qquad T_{u,v}B=T_{u,B^*v},$$\n\nwith the Hilbert adjoint $B^*$ ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]).\n\n(b) Let $T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be trace class and let $T=\\sum_jT_{u_j,v_j}$ be a nuclear representation, i.e. the partial sums converge to $T$ in operator norm and $\\sum_j\\|u_j\\|\\,\\|v_j\\|<\\infty$ ([[lem-nuclear-series-characterizes-trace-norm]], [[def-trace-class-operator]]). Then for every bounded $B$\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(BT)=\\sum_j\\langle Bu_j,v_j\\rangle,\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(TB)=\\sum_j\\langle u_j,B^*v_j\\rangle,$$\n\nboth series converging absolutely with $\\bigl|\\operatorname{tr}(BT)\\bigr|\\le\\|B\\|\\sum_j\\|u_j\\|\\,\\|v_j\\|$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(BT)=\\operatorname{tr}(TB)$ ([[thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent]]). In particular $\\operatorname{tr}(BT)=\\operatorname{tr}(TB)$ for every trace-class $T$ and bounded $B$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(T_{u,v})=\\langle u,v\\rangle$. No cyclic permutation of unbounded operators is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space with the pairing linear in the first argument ([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), and for $u,v\\in H$ let\n\n$$T_{u,v}x:=\\langle x,v\\rangle u\\qquad(x\\in H).$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $T_{u,v}\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ with $\\|T_{u,v}\\|=\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-operator-norm]]), and the assignment $(u,v)\\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is linear in $u$ and conjugate-linear in $v$;\n\n(b) $T_{u,v}$ is trace class ([[def-trace-class-operator]]) with $\\|T_{u,v}\\|_1=\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(T_{u,v})=\\langle u,v\\rangle$;\n\n(c) $T_{u,v}^*=T_{v,u}$ ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]); $T_{x,x}$ is a positive bounded operator in the sense of [[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]], with $\\langle T_{x,x}y,y\\rangle=|\\langle y,x\\rangle|^2\\ge0$ for every $y\\in H$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,x})=\\|x\\|^2$, $\\|T_{x,x}\\|_1=\\|x\\|^2$;\n\n(d) for bounded $B\\in\\mathcal B(H)$, $BT_{u,v}=T_{Bu,v}$ and $T_{u,v}B=T_{u,B^*v}$;\n\n(e) for trace-class $S$, $ST_{u,v}=T_{Su,v}$, $T_{u,v}S=T_{u,S^*v}$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(ST_{x,y})=\\langle Sx,y\\rangle=\\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,y}S)$;\n\n(f) the map $(u,v)\\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is continuous $H\\times H\\to\\mathcal S_1(H)$ for the trace norm, so in particular $v\\mapsto T_{v,v}$ and $v\\mapsto T_{u,v}$ are trace-norm continuous for fixed $u$.\n\n*Scope.* Only the rank-one calculus is asserted; no spectral, compactness or ideal statement beyond (b) is claimed, and no physical interpretation is attached.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space with the pairing linear in the first argument ([[def-hilbert-space]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), and for $u,v\\in H$ let\n\n$$T_{u,v}x:=\\langle x,v\\rangle u\\qquad(x\\in H).$$\n\nThen:\n\n(a) $T_{u,v}\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ with $\\|T_{u,v}\\|=\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-operator-norm]]), and the assignment $(u,v)\\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is linear in $u$ and conjugate-linear in $v$;\n\n(b) $T_{u,v}$ is trace class ([[def-trace-class-operator]]) with $\\|T_{u,v}\\|_1=\\|u\\|\\,\\|v\\|$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(T_{u,v})=\\langle u,v\\rangle$;\n\n(c) $T_{u,v}^*=T_{v,u}$ ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]); $T_{x,x}$ is a positive bounded operator in the sense of [[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]], with $\\langle T_{x,x}y,y\\rangle=|\\langle y,x\\rangle|^2\\ge0$ for every $y\\in H$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,x})=\\|x\\|^2$, $\\|T_{x,x}\\|_1=\\|x\\|^2$;\n\n(d) for bounded $B\\in\\mathcal B(H)$, $BT_{u,v}=T_{Bu,v}$ and $T_{u,v}B=T_{u,B^*v}$;\n\n(e) for trace-class $S$, $ST_{u,v}=T_{Su,v}$, $T_{u,v}S=T_{u,S^*v}$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(ST_{x,y})=\\langle Sx,y\\rangle=\\operatorname{tr}(T_{x,y}S)$;\n\n(f) the map $(u,v)\\mapsto T_{u,v}$ is continuous $H\\times H\\to\\mathcal S_1(H)$ for the trace norm, so in particular $v\\mapsto T_{v,v}$ and $v\\mapsto T_{u,v}$ are trace-norm continuous for fixed $u$.\n\n*Scope.* Only the rank-one calculus is asserted; no spectral, compactness or ideal statement beyond (b) is claimed, and no physical interpretation is attached.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-trace-class-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ and\n$K$ be Hilbert spaces over the same field $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$\n([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H,K)$ be a compact operator ([[def-compact-linear-operator]])\nwith zero-padded singular-value sequence $(s_n(T))_{n\\ge1}$\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]).\n\n**Trace class.** The operator $T$ is **trace class** when\n$$\\sum_{n\\ge1}s_n(T):=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb N}s_{m+1}(T)<+\\infty .$$\nThus the series in the zero-based convention of\n[[def-series-and-absolute-convergence-in-a-normed-space]] is formed from the\nexplicit sequence $(s_{m+1}(T))_{m\\in\\mathbb N}$. In that case its **trace\nnorm** is\n$$\\|T\\|_1:=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb N}s_{m+1}(T)\\in[0,+\\infty),$$\nand $\\|T\\|_1$ is also written $\\|T\\|_{\\mathrm{tr}}$. The set of trace-class\noperators $H\\to K$ is written $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$.\n\n**Immediate consequences.** Since $0\\le s_n(T)\\le s_1(T)=\\|T\\|$\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]), the terms\nare nonnegative and $\\|T\\|_1\\ge\\|T\\|\\ge0$ whenever $T$ is trace class; the zero\noperator is trace class with $\\|0\\|_1=0$. When $T$ has finite rank $r$ the\nsequence is zero-padded, the series is the finite sum\n$\\sum_{n=1}^r s_n(T)=\\sum_{\\lambda>0}\\lambda\\dim_{\\mathbb F} E_\\lambda(|T|)$ over the finitely\nmany positive eigenvalues of $|T|$ counted with multiplicity ([[def-dimension]]),\nand every finite-rank operator is compact\n([[lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact]]) and therefore trace class; in particular every\noperator with finite-dimensional range and every rank-one operator is trace\nclass. If $T$ has infinite rank then $s_n(T)>0$ for every $n$ and the series\n$\\sum_{n\\ge1}s_n(T)$ converges in the summable case. By the necessary\ncondition for convergence of a scalar series ([[lem-nth-term-test]]), a\ntrace-class operator with infinite rank has $s_n(T)\\to0$, hence\nis a norm limit of finite-rank operators\n([[thm-singular-value-decomposition-for-compact-operators]]).\n\n**Choice accounting.** Trace class is defined through the singular values of\n[[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]], whose\nconstruction uses $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ through the countable selection of finite\northonormal bases of the eigenspaces of $|T|$ and the spectral theorem; no\nHilbert basis of the ambient space, and no stronger choice, is used here.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-trace-of-a-trace-class-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\nreal or complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]), let $T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$\nbe a trace-class operator on $H$ ([[def-trace-class-operator]]) and let $E$ be a\nHilbert basis of $H$, **supplied as data**\n([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]];\nexistence of such a basis is not asserted here). The **trace of $T$ relative to\n$E$** is\n$$\\operatorname{tr}_E(T):=\\sum_{e\\in E}\\langle Te,e\\rangle,$$\nthe sum of the scalar family $(\\langle Te,e\\rangle)_{e\\in E}$ in the\nfinite-subset-net sense of\n[[def-square-summable-family-on-an-arbitrary-index-set]].\n\n**The family is absolutely summable, uniformly in $E$.** Let\n$T=\\sum_js_j\\langle\\cdot,e_j\\rangle f_j$ be the singular-value series\n([[thm-singular-value-decomposition-for-compact-operators]],\n[[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]) with\n$(e_j)_{j\\in J}$, $(f_j)_{j\\in J}$ orthonormal and $\\sum_js_j=\\|T\\|_1<+\\infty$\n([[def-trace-class-operator]]). For every $e\\in H$ the series\n$\\langle Te,e\\rangle=\\sum_{j}s_j\\langle e,e_j\\rangle\\langle f_j,e\\rangle$\nconverges absolutely, because the moduli are bounded by\n$s_1\\sum_j|\\langle e,e_j\\rangle\\langle f_j,e\\rangle|\\le s_1\\|e\\|^2$ by\nCauchy–Schwarz and Bessel\n([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]],\n[[lem-finite-bessel-inequality]]). Consequently, for every finite\n$F\\subseteq E$, using the interchange of finite sums with the nonnegative\nfinite-subset supremum over $j$ and then finite Cauchy–Schwarz and Bessel twice,\n$$\\sum_{e\\in F}|\\langle Te,e\\rangle|\\le\\sum_{e\\in F}\\sum_{j}s_j|\\langle e,e_j\\rangle|\\,|\\langle f_j,e\\rangle|=\\sum_{j}s_j\\sum_{e\\in F}|\\langle e,e_j\\rangle|\\,|\\langle f_j,e\\rangle|\\le\\sum_{j}s_j\\Bigl(\\sum_{e\\in F}|\\langle e,e_j\\rangle|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2}\\Bigl(\\sum_{e\\in F}|\\langle f_j,e\\rangle|^2\\Bigr)^{1/2}\\le\\sum_{j}s_j=\\|T\\|_1 ,$$\nbecause for each $j$ the two finite coefficient sums are bounded by\n$\\|e_j\\|=1$ and $\\|f_j\\|=1$ (finite Bessel). Hence the finite subsums of\n$|(\\langle Te,e\\rangle)|_{e\\in E}$ are bounded by $\\|T\\|_1$, the family is\nabsolutely summable, and an absolutely summable family of scalars is summable in\nZF with $|\\sum_{e\\in E}\\langle Te,e\\rangle|\\le\\|T\\|_1$\n([[def-square-summable-family-on-an-arbitrary-index-set]]). This justifies the\nnotation $\\operatorname{tr}_E(T)$ before any basis-independence statement.\n\n**Choice accounting and status.** Only a supplied basis $E$ and the singular\nvalues of $T$ are used; no Hilbert basis of $H$ is assumed to exist, and the\nnext theorem proves that $\\operatorname{tr}_E(T)$ does not depend on the\nsupplied basis and equals the basis-free trace of $T$\n([[thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\nreal or complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be trace class ([[def-trace-class-operator]]). Then:\n\n1. for every **nuclear representation** $Tx=\\sum_j\\langle x,u_j\\rangle v_j$ of\n   $T$ (operator-norm convergence of the partial sums,\n   $\\sum_j\\|u_j\\|\\|v_j\\|<+\\infty$), the scalar series $\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle$\n   converges absolutely and\n   $$\\Bigl|\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle\\Bigr|\\le\\sum_j\\|u_j\\|\\,\\|v_j\\| ;$$\n2. the sum $\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle$ depends only on $T$; denoting it\n   $\\operatorname{tr}(T)$, one has\n   $$\\operatorname{tr}(T)=\\sum_j\\langle v_j,u_j\\rangle$$\n   for **every** nuclear representation of $T$. This defines $\\operatorname{tr}(T)$\n   without assuming that $H$ has a Hilbert basis;\n3. for every supplied Hilbert basis $E$ of $H$,\n   $\\operatorname{tr}_E(T)=\\operatorname{tr}(T)$\n   ([[def-trace-of-a-trace-class-operator]]);\n4. the trace is linear in the trace-class variable and bounded by the trace\n   norm: for trace-class $S,T$ and scalars $a,b$,\n   $\\operatorname{tr}(aS+bT)=a\\operatorname{tr}(S)+b\\operatorname{tr}(T)$ and\n   $|\\operatorname{tr}(T)|\\le\\|T\\|_1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice, and let $H$ be a real or complex Hilbert space with $T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ a bounded linear operator and $T^*$ its Hilbert adjoint ([[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]], [[def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators]]).\n\n- $T$ is **self-adjoint** when $T^*=T$;\n- $T$ is **positive** when $\\langle Tx,x\\rangle$ is a real number in $[0,+\\infty)$ for every $x\\in H$;\n- $T$ is **unitary** when $T^*T=TT^*=I$, the identity operator;\n- $T$ is **normal** when $T^*T=TT^*$.\n\nThe definitions are read over either scalar field with the same inner product. **Two immediate consequences.** Every self-adjoint operator is normal, because $T^*T=T^2=TT^*$ when $T^*=T$. Every unitary operator is normal, because its defining identity says exactly that $T^*T$ and $TT^*$ are both the identity. Positivity is a condition on the *values* of the quadratic form and therefore forces those values to be real, which for a complex Hilbert space does not follow from boundedness alone; a positive operator on a complex Hilbert space is in fact self-adjoint, but that is proved later and is not assumed here.\n\n**Consistency of the vocabulary.** The identity operator is self-adjoint, positive and unitary, and the zero operator is self-adjoint and positive; on the one-dimensional Hilbert space $\\mathbb C$ the operator $\\lambda I$ is self-adjoint exactly when $\\lambda$ is real, unitary exactly when $|\\lambda|=1$, and normal for every scalar $\\lambda$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-trace-class-is-a-two-sided-banach-operator-ideal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$, $K$\nand $L$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces ([[def-hilbert-space]]). Then:\n\n1. the trace-class operators $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ ([[def-trace-class-operator]])\n   form a linear subspace of $\\mathcal B(H,K)$ on which $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ is a norm,\n   and\n   $$\\|T\\|\\le\\|T\\|_1\\qquad\\text{for every }T\\in\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$$\n   ([[def-operator-norm]]);\n2. if $T\\in\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ and $A\\in\\mathcal B(K,L)$, $B\\in\\mathcal B(H_0,H)$\n   are bounded linear operators on Hilbert spaces $H_0,L$, then\n   $ATB\\in\\mathcal S_1(H_0,L)$ and\n   $$\\|ATB\\|_1\\le\\|A\\|\\,\\|T\\|_1\\,\\|B\\| ;$$\n3. $(\\mathcal S_1(H,K),\\|\\cdot\\|_1)$ is a Banach space: every\n   $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$-Cauchy sequence in $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ has a limit in\n   $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$ to which it converges in $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$\n   ([[def-banach-space]], [[def-norm-and-normed-space]],\n   [[def-metric-space]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each $a,r$, $M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}$ is self-adjoint because $\\langle M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}x,y\\rangle=\\langle M_{a,r}x,M_{a,r}y\\rangle=\\langle x,M_{a,r}^*M_{a,r}y\\rangle$, and its quadratic form is $\\|M_{a,r}x\\|^2\\ge0$. Hence $E_a\\ge0$. Normalization gives $I_H-E_a=\\sum_{b\\ne a}\\sum_rM_{b,r}^*M_{b,r}\\ge0$, including empty sums, so $E_a$ is an effect.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [F7], each $M_{a,r}\\rho M_{a,r}^*$ is trace class, so the finite sum $\\mathcal J_a(\\rho)$ is trace class. For all $x,y\\in H$, $\\langle M_{a,r}\\rho M_{a,r}^*x,y\\rangle=\\langle\\rho M_{a,r}^*x,M_{a,r}^*y\\rangle=\\langle M_{a,r}^*x,\\rho M_{a,r}^*y\\rangle=\\langle x,M_{a,r}\\rho M_{a,r}^*y\\rangle$ because $\\rho=\\rho^*$ and $M_{a,r}^*$ is the Hilbert adjoint. Also $\\langle M_{a,r}\\rho M_{a,r}^*x,x\\rangle=\\langle\\rho M_{a,r}^*x,M_{a,r}^*x\\rangle\\ge0$. Finite summation shows $\\mathcal J_a(\\rho)$ is self-adjoint and positive.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
